00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:53.452
Jennifer Lawrence
and Timothee Chalamet

00:05:53.452 --> 00:05:55.021
and we can all use
just a little --

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:57.623
just a little bit more Chalamet.

00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:01.493
Also, imagine finally reemerging
from six months of quarantine,

00:06:01.493 --> 00:06:04.062
only to be told you have
to go back inside

00:06:04.062 --> 00:06:05.398
for a different thing.

00:06:05.398 --> 00:06:07.800
"Oh, is it COVID surging again?"

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.468
Um, no.
It's not that.

00:06:09.468 --> 00:06:12.371
It's -- Ooh!
How do we put this?

00:06:12.371 --> 00:06:15.174
The sky is on fire.

00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:17.443
More than 3 million acres have
burned in California, alone.

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:20.112
In Oregon, 40,000 people
have been evacuated

00:06:20.112 --> 00:06:22.681
and half a million are
in potential danger zones.

00:06:22.681 --> 00:06:25.150
And there is no doubt
that the frequency

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:27.453
and intensity of these
extreme weather events

00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:29.489
have been supercharged
by climate change.

00:06:29.489 --> 00:06:31.957
New research shows that the
Earth hasn't warmed this fast

00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:33.459
in tens of millions of years.

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:38.264
L.A. County just set a new
record temperature of 121°.

00:06:38.264 --> 00:06:39.465
That's insane.

00:06:39.465 --> 00:06:41.467
Scientists and experts
have been clear

00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:42.735
that what we're seeing right now

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:44.770
is the direct result
of climate change.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:49.475
-We just had two unprecedented
heat waves back-to-back,

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:51.510 line:0%
some of the hottest temperatures
ever experienced

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.913 line:0%
in the Southwest
and in California,

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:56.015 line:0%
both of them basically
unprecedented,

00:06:56.015 --> 00:06:58.117 line:0%
made worse by climate change.

00:06:58.117 --> 00:07:00.353 line:0%
And that really dries
out the foliage.

00:07:00.353 --> 00:07:02.387 line:0%
It dries out the brush.
It dries out the trees.

00:07:02.387 --> 00:07:06.725 line:0%
What that does is it adds energy
and heat to the atmosphere.

00:07:06.725 --> 00:07:08.127 line:0%
That dries out the brush.

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:09.361 line:0%
It dries out the atmosphere.

00:07:09.361 --> 00:07:11.096 line:0%
It causes a moisture gap,

00:07:11.096 --> 00:07:13.632 line:0%
or moisture deficit,
in the atmosphere

00:07:13.632 --> 00:07:16.068 line:0%
and research shows that
that moisture deficit

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:18.637 line:0%
can explain almost all
of the increase

00:07:18.637 --> 00:07:21.207 line:0%
in burned area since the 1970s.

00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:23.442
-That's right,
climate change has produced

00:07:23.442 --> 00:07:26.512
a moisture gap that's
making wildfires worse.

00:07:26.512 --> 00:07:29.515
Of course, a moisture gap is
also what you call the distance

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:31.517
between Trump's lips
and a bottle of water.

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:32.851
[ As Trump ]
Damn you, water. C'mere.

00:07:32.851 --> 00:07:35.120
Stop -- Stop playing
co coy, water.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:38.323
You guys, we gotta get someone
to close the moisture gap.

00:07:38.323 --> 00:07:41.093
Can we put Fauci on it,
to work on a solution?

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:42.094
Why?
What's he working on?

00:07:42.094 --> 00:07:43.395
COVID, still?

00:07:43.395 --> 00:07:44.463
Alright.

00:07:44.463 --> 00:07:46.498
Six of the top 20
largest wildfires

00:07:46.498 --> 00:07:48.968
in California's history have
all happened just this year

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:51.337
and, if we don't take
mass collective action

00:07:51.337 --> 00:07:54.106
to address climate change now,
things are only gonna get worse.

00:07:54.106 --> 00:07:56.408
-Unfortunately,
climate change is happening

00:07:56.408 --> 00:07:58.511
right now in California

00:07:58.511 --> 00:08:01.213 line:0%
and we've been lengthening
the fire season

00:08:01.213 --> 00:08:04.616 line:0%
quite considerably
as we warm up the planet.

00:08:04.616 --> 00:08:06.886 line:0%
We know, because of research
from scientists,

00:08:06.886 --> 00:08:11.190 line:0%
that we have 500%
more risk for wildfires

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:14.894 line:0%
during this climate-changed
world than we would have before

00:08:14.894 --> 00:08:17.997 line:0%
and that's because
it's really hot and dry

00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:19.832 line:0%
because we have had droughts.

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:21.133 line:0%
And, of course, that drought,

00:08:21.133 --> 00:08:22.501 line:0%
which is caused
by climate change,

00:08:22.501 --> 00:08:24.870 line:0%
has led to a lot
of vegetation dying,

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.105 line:0%
meaning there's a lot
of brush lying around

00:08:27.105 --> 00:08:29.208 line:0%
that can easily light up.

00:08:29.208 --> 00:08:32.512 line:0%
The fact is, climate change
is the real culprit

00:08:32.512 --> 00:08:34.313 line:0%
behind what
we're seeing right now.

00:08:34.313 --> 00:08:36.715 line:0%
-Should we expect
that trend to continue?

00:08:36.715 --> 00:08:38.517 line:0%
-Unfortunately, we should.

00:08:38.517 --> 00:08:40.052 line:0%
What scientists are telling us

00:08:40.052 --> 00:08:43.222 line:0%
is that we are entering
a period of mega fires.

00:08:43.222 --> 00:08:44.924
-I love when scientists
say the hell with it

00:08:44.924 --> 00:08:47.159
and give something
a simple-to-understand,

00:08:47.159 --> 00:08:48.527
yet terrifying, name,

00:08:48.527 --> 00:08:51.731
just so we might pay a little
bit of attention to it.

00:08:51.731 --> 00:08:53.533
Most of the time,
scientists use jargon,

00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:55.467
like "anthropogenic
climate change"

00:08:55.467 --> 00:08:58.204
or "atmospheric aerosols,"
but, when they discover

00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:00.272
something really bad,
they just give it a name

00:09:00.272 --> 00:09:02.141
that makes you
[bleep] your pants.

00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:03.942
"So, as you can see here,
rising heat combines

00:09:03.942 --> 00:09:06.311
with wind turbulence
to form eddes of air

00:09:06.311 --> 00:09:08.680
that create a vortex
of combustible gases

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:10.216
and hexapod invertebrates,

00:09:10.216 --> 00:09:11.851
or what we in the science
community call...

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:18.057
We all laughed when Syfy
came out with "Sharknado,"

00:09:18.057 --> 00:09:19.492
but, if they make another one,

00:09:19.492 --> 00:09:21.426
I'm gonna be watching it
with a notepad.

00:09:21.426 --> 00:09:23.662
Sharks can penetrate
cinder block.

00:09:23.662 --> 00:09:26.131
Okay. Good to know.
Good to know.

00:09:26.131 --> 00:09:28.066
So while a deadly pandemic
continued to grow

00:09:28.066 --> 00:09:29.334
in at least 11 states,

00:09:29.334 --> 00:09:31.537
with the nation's
top infectious disease expert

00:09:31.537 --> 00:09:33.071
calling the situation
disturbing,

00:09:33.071 --> 00:09:35.341
and historic wildfires scorched

00:09:35.341 --> 00:09:37.109
millions of acres
across the West,

00:09:37.109 --> 00:09:39.545
turning the sky orange
and making the air

00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:41.313
